        "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <title>Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) - Mathematical Preliminaries</title>\n  <script>window.MathJax = { tex: { inlineMath: [['$', '$'], ['\\\\(', '\\\\)']], displayMath: [['$$', '$$'], ['\\\\[', '\\\\]']] } };</script>\n  <script async src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-mml-chtml.js\"></script>\n  <style>\n    @page { size: 8.5in 11in; margin: 0; }\n    * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n    body {\n      width: 8.5in;\n      min-height: 11in;\n      margin: 0 auto;\n      padding: 0.43in 0.53in 0.28in;\n      color: #000;\n      background: #fff;\n      font-family: \"Times New Roman\", Times, serif;\n      font-size: 15.8pt;\n      line-height: 1.14;\n    }\n    .running-head {\n      display: flex;\n      justify-content: space-between;\n      align-items: baseline;\n      margin: 0 0 0.36in;\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n      font-size: 14pt;\n      letter-spacing: 0.02em;\n    }\n    .running-head .right { font-variant: small-caps; }\n    h1, h2 {\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n      margin: 0;\n      font-weight: 700;\n    }\n    h1 {\n      font-size: 24pt;\n      line-height: 1.1;\n      margin-bottom: 0.22in;\n    }\n    h1 .num { display: inline-block; width: 0.46in; }\n    h2 {\n      font-size: 20pt;\n      line-height: 1.1;\n      margin: 0.19in 0 0.18in;\n    }\n    h2 .num { display: inline-block; width: 0.48in; }\n    p { margin: 0 0 0.16in; text-align: left; }\n    .equation {\n      position: relative;\n      min-height: 0.34in;\n      margin: 0.18in 0 0.18in;\n      text-align: center;\n      font-size: 16pt;\n    }\n    .equation .label {\n      position: absolute;\n      right: 0;\n      top: 0;\n    }\n    .examples {\n      margin: 0.38in 0 0.32in;\n      font-size: 15.5pt;\n    }\n    .example-row {\n      display: grid;\n      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.16in;\n      column-gap: 0.08in;\n      align-items: baseline;\n      margin: 0.03in 0;\n    }\n    .example-row .expr { text-align: center; white-space: nowrap; }\n    .example-row .kind { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }\n    .example-row .eqno { position: absolute; right: 0; }\n    .examples-wrap { position: relative; }\n    .examples-wrap .eqno { position: absolute; right: 0; bottom: 0.03in; }\n    .footnote {\n      border-top: 1px solid #000;\n      width: 3.05in;\n      margin-top: 0.04in;\n      padding-top: 0.02in;\n      font-size: 10.5pt;\n      line-height: 1.05;\n    }\n    .page-number {\n      text-align: center;\n      font-size: 13pt;\n      margin-top: 0.08in;\n    }\n    a { color: #0000ee; text-decoration: none; }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <header class=\"running-head\">\n    <div>FIPS 197</div>\n    <div class=\"right\">Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)</div>\n  </header>\n\n  <main>\n    <h1><span class=\"num\">4.</span>Mathematical Preliminaries</h1>\n\n    <p>For some transformations of the AES algorithms specified in Sec. <a href=\"#\">5</a>, each byte in the state array is interpreted as one of the 256 elements of a finite field, also known as a Galois Field, denoted by GF(2<sup>8</sup>). <sup><a href=\"#fn1\">1</a></sup></p>\n\n    <p>In order to define addition and multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>), each byte {<i>b</i><sub>7</sub> <i>b</i><sub>6</sub> <i>b</i><sub>5</sub> <i>b</i><sub>4</sub> <i>b</i><sub>3</sub> <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>} is interpreted as a polynomial, denoted by <i>b</i>(<i>x</i>), as follows:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"equation\">$b(x)=b_7x^7+b_6x^6+b_5x^5+b_4x^4+b_3x^3+b_2x^2+b_1x+b_0.$<span class=\"label\">(4.1)</span></div>\n\n    <p>For example, {01100011} is represented by the polynomial <i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>5</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1.</p>\n\n    <h2><span class=\"num\">4.1</span>Addition in GF(2<sup>8</sup>)</h2>\n\n    <p>In order to add two elements in the finite field GF(2<sup>8</sup>), the coefficients of the polynomials that represent the elements are added modulo 2 (i.e., with the exclusive-OR operation, denoted by &oplus;), so that 1 &oplus; 1 = 0, 1 &oplus; 0 = 1, and 0 &oplus; 0 = 0.</p>\n\n    <p>Equivalently, two bytes can be added by applying the exclusive-OR operation to each pair of corresponding bits in the bytes. Thus, the sum of {<i>a</i><sub>7</sub> <i>a</i><sub>6</sub> <i>a</i><sub>5</sub> <i>a</i><sub>4</sub> <i>a</i><sub>3</sub> <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> <i>a</i><sub>1</sub> <i>a</i><sub>0</sub>} and {<i>b</i><sub>7</sub> <i>b</i><sub>6</sub> <i>b</i><sub>5</sub> <i>b</i><sub>4</sub> <i>b</i><sub>3</sub> <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>} is {<i>a</i><sub>7</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>7</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>6</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>6</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>5</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>5</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>4</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>4</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>3</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>3</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>2</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>1</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>1</sub>&nbsp; <i>a</i><sub>0</sub> &oplus; <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>}. (In Section <a href=\"#\">5.1.4</a>, this definition is extended to words.)</p>\n\n    <p>For example, the following three representations of addition are equivalent:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"examples-wrap examples\">\n      <div class=\"example-row\">\n        <div class=\"expr\">(<i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1) + (<i>x</i><sup>7</sup> + <i>x</i> + 1) = <i>x</i><sup>7</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>6</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup></div>\n        <div class=\"kind\">(polynomial)</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"example-row\">\n        <div class=\"expr\">{01010111} &oplus; {10000011} = {11010100}</div>\n        <div class=\"kind\">(binary)</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"example-row\">\n        <div class=\"expr\">{57} &oplus; {83} = {d4}</div>\n        <div class=\"kind\">(hexadecimal).</div>\n      </div>\n      <span class=\"eqno\">(4.2)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <p>Because the coefficients of the polynomials are reduced modulo 2, the coefficient 1 is equivalent to the coefficient &minus;1, so addition is equivalent to subtraction. For example, <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> represents the same finite field element as <i>x</i><sup>4</sup> &minus; <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, &minus;<i>x</i><sup>4</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, and &minus;<i>x</i><sup>4</sup> &minus; <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>. Similarly, the sum of any element with itself is the zero element.</p>\n\n    <h2><span class=\"num\">4.2</span>Multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>)</h2>\n\n    <p>The symbol &bull; denotes multiplication in GF(2<sup>8</sup>). Conceptually, this multiplication is defined on two bytes in two steps: 1) the two polynomials that represent the bytes are multiplied as polynomials, and 2) the resulting polynomial is reduced modulo the following fixed polynomial:</p>\n\n    <div class=\"equation\">$m(x)=x^8+x^4+x^3+x+1.$<span class=\"label\">(4.3)</span></div>\n\n    <p>Within both steps, the individual coefficients of the polynomials are reduced modulo 2.</p>\n  </main>\n\n  <footer>\n    <div class=\"footnote\" id=\"fn1\"><sup>1</sup>Information about the properties of finite fields can be found in textbooks, such as Michael Artin’s <i>Algebra</i> <a href=\"#\">[4]</a>.</div>\n    <div class=\"page-number\">8</div>\n  </footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n"
